From dfa016ca1b794746dc16e2262128448d643edfd4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stew Forster <ci@rdna-boosts.invalid>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 10:40:31 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 00/15] rdna-boosts: block 00: foundation - band-uniformity,
 Meta-wrapper and allocator fixes

Groundwork block (multiple base-level concerns, by design):
- FA KV-split width invariance (launch_fattn, n_q <= 8)
- shared-NextN MTP heads keep their own KV (is_mem_shared gate)
- CPU llamafile_sgemm routes n <= 8 to vec_dot (decode/verify band unity)
- single-device -sm tensor skips the Meta wrapper
- meta-buffer compute-container headroom 16 -> 128 (recurrent snapshot views)
- multi-ubatch logits readback ordering (async D2H race)
- ggml-alloc: release view sources whose views are never consumed (W4)
- ggml-alloc: reuse the recorded layout only for the graph it was computed for
  (graph fingerprint; delivery repo issue #183)

Sources: old 00, 05, 07, 09a(compute headroom), 06(readback), 15(W4).

Also carries two generic upstream foundation changes:
- the upstream MUL_MAT_ID duplicate-ids fix (upstream/UPSTREAM-PR-mul-mat-id-dup.patch):
  mm_ids_helper keeps every occurrence of a repeated expert id as its own compact row,
  the MMQ/MMF column-tile grids loop over the expanded per-expert column list, and the
  host sorted fallback emits every match.  Fixes the test-backend-ops MUL_MAT_ID dup=1
  failures and the quantize_mmq_q8_1 page fault.  Applied here so every later MUL_MAT_ID
  block builds on correct duplicate handling.
- cache the pre-existing upstream env reads that sit on hot paths in function-local
  statics: GGML_CUDA_CUBLAS_COMPUTE_TYPE (per cuBLAS matmul),
  GGML_CUDA_ENABLE_UNIFIED_MEMORY (per device allocation), GGML_CUDA_REGISTER_HOST,
  GGML_CUDA_NO_PINNED, LLAMA_GRAPH_INPUT_DEBUG (per graph input) and LLAMA_BATCH_DEBUG
  (per batch).  getenv() is expensive on Windows.

Also fixes delivery repo issue #183.  ggml_gallocr_alloc_graph() re-applied the recorded
node_allocs/leaf_allocs positionally and invalidated them only on a node/leaf count or a per-node
size change, so a graph with the same node count but a different node order, op/shape set or output
marking inherited a layout computed for a different graph.  In-graph sampling (build_sampling +
ggml_build_forward_select, one chain per sequence x output row) is exactly such a graph: selecting
another sequence's chain keeps the node count and changes which top_k tensors are outputs, so with
the second scheduler on a --parallel server the newly active candidate-id tensors all landed on one
recycled block and every output row read the last computed ids.  DFlash drafting therefore produced
correct drafts only for the first slot that served a request.  The allocator now records an FNV-1a
fingerprint of the graph its layout belongs to (node/leaf order, op, type, ne[], view_src,
INPUT/OUTPUT/COMPUTE flags, node/leaf split) and re-reserves on a mismatch; it only ever adds a
re-reserve, never skips one.

Also extend the -Wshadow sweep to the remaining upstream shadowing declarations the clang
build reports (all present at the fork point): step35.cpp per-layer n_embd_k_gqa/n_embd_v_gqa,
llama-moe-cache.cpp init_ctx, llama-vocab.cpp _try_copy, llama-quant.cpp layer_info, and
llama-model.cpp make_gpu_buft_list / get_tensor.

Fold (r86): beta 13, meta-hot-dispatcher (issue #184).  The split-state dispatcher is back to
the exact pre-#170 shape and the #170 alias/host resolution lives in a noinline,
cold helper.  Measured zero (-0.06 %, greedy text identical, 4 concurrent
streams on 2x R9700); the dispatcher drops 454 -> 287 B.
